1
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:11,430
We go to work. We pay bills. We scroll. We compete.

2
00:00:12,030 --> 00:00:18,053
We call exhaustion adulthood, anxiety responsibility,

3
00:00:18,653 --> 00:00:25,063
isolation independence, and endless accumulation success.

4
00:00:25,663 --> 00:00:29,530
We speak proudly of freedom.

5
00:00:30,130 --> 00:00:37,830
Then we go quiet when exercising it might cost us our place.

6
00:00:40,230 --> 00:00:47,703
Here in America, we are very comfortable calling ourselves advanced.

7
00:00:48,303 --> 00:00:57,323
But advanced at what? Producing more? Predicting behavior? Getting people to comply?

8
00:00:57,923 --> 00:01:05,590
I am asking whether the person is developing along with the machinery.

9
00:01:06,190 --> 00:01:17,240
Are we becoming more capable of examining our beliefs, choosing deliberately, and caring for one another?

10
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:31,823
Because if those capacities are diminishing, I call that regression. Our machines cannot develop our awareness on our behalf.

11
00:01:34,423 --> 00:01:39,093
Beneath all of this is a moral erosion.

12
00:01:39,693 --> 00:01:44,653
We are taught that anyone can be anything.

13
00:01:45,253 --> 00:01:53,210
It sounds like freedom. It is unrealistic, and it is no moral foundation.

14
00:01:53,810 --> 00:02:01,897
We learn what we could become. We are rarely asked what is good.

15
00:02:04,097 --> 00:02:13,890
And the establishment has to answer for its part in this. The scientific community included.

16
00:02:14,490 --> 00:02:24,573
Scientific institutions are part of society. They do not float above money, prestige, or power.

17
00:02:25,173 --> 00:02:35,063
What gets funded? Which questions become respectable? And whose purposes does the work serve?

18
00:02:35,663 --> 00:02:46,810
When your research makes people easier to predict, persuade, and control, whose freedom are you expanding?

19
00:02:47,410 --> 00:02:56,913
You cannot help build those tools and declare their consequences somebody else’s problem.

20
00:02:57,513 --> 00:03:07,500
Nor can you present your assumptions as established knowledge simply because you have a title.

21
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:19,537
Show us the evidence. Explain its limits. Respect for expertise should not require the surrender of judgment.

22
00:03:20,137 --> 00:03:30,640
And helping a worker endure more does not answer whether more should be demanded of them.

23
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:42,440
Civilization does not arrive inside you labeled civilization. It arrives as ordinary life.

24
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:53,317
Through what your family praises, what your school rewards, and what your employer will tolerate.

25
00:03:53,917 --> 00:04:03,710
It teaches you what to want, whom to believe, and which questions might cost you your place.

26
00:04:04,310 --> 00:04:11,687
Eventually, its expectations begin to sound like your own thoughts.

27
00:04:12,287 --> 00:04:22,757
By the time you question the world, much of that world is already participating in the questioning.

28
00:04:23,357 --> 00:04:29,090
You inherited a Painting already begun. I did too.

29
00:04:29,690 --> 00:04:39,483
You tell yourself you are fine. But at night, you rehearse the words you were afraid to say.

30
00:04:40,083 --> 00:04:54,033
Then, in the morning, you go back to proving your worth. Somewhere along the way, earning a living became proving that you deserve one.

31
00:04:54,633 --> 00:05:07,650
I am not describing this to look down on the person caught inside it. I am asking how we help one another get free.

32
00:05:10,650 --> 00:05:20,057
An arrangement can benefit from the very condition that is hurting the people inside it.

33
00:05:20,657 --> 00:05:31,900
If insecurity keeps you buying and fear keeps you obedient, somebody can profit from keeping you unsettled.

34
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:41,457
No one has to coordinate every part of it. The rewards can keep it going.

35
00:05:43,857 --> 00:05:52,200
In the American Millennial Manifesto, I call this extractive logic the Leech.

36
00:05:52,800 --> 00:06:03,947
The Manifesto looks outward: at how wealth and power concentrate, and how dependence shapes everyday life.

37
00:06:04,547 --> 00:06:11,537
The Leech names the arrangement. Conditioning helps it survive.

38
00:06:12,137 --> 00:06:19,740
Digital Organism Theory looks inward, at that conditioning.

39
00:06:21,940 --> 00:06:32,893
DOT asks what it would mean for the person inside that arrangement to develop, rather than merely adapt.

40
00:06:33,493 --> 00:06:44,673
Unless we address our own conditioning, the pain continues, and so does the lack of fulfillment.

41
00:06:45,273 --> 00:06:52,263
The most immediate fact of your existence is not a measurement.

42
00:06:52,863 --> 00:07:02,200
It is that you are here to experience one. The observer belongs in the inquiry.

43
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:11,460
Feeling must be treated as data. But feeling is not automatically truth.

44
00:07:12,060 --> 00:07:21,177
Digital Organism Theory is the framework I have been building around these questions.

45
00:07:21,777 --> 00:07:34,213
It is a construction, not a revelation. Examine it. I am not asking you to replace their authority with mine.

46
00:07:36,413 --> 00:07:41,760
[DOT proposes] In DOT, the purpose is expansion of awareness.

47
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:53,990
I do not mean collecting more information. I mean becoming more capable of seeing, understanding, and choosing.

48
00:07:54,590 --> 00:08:07,960
That requires mental focus. The ability to stay with experience long enough to examine it, including what you would rather avoid.

49
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:22,027
[DOT proposes] In DOT, that capacity depends on internal coherence—on becoming less divided by fear, self-deception, and the defense of identity.

50
00:08:22,627 --> 00:08:33,580
Coherence is not certainty. You can be very certain and very wrong. There has to be room for correction.

51
00:08:34,180 --> 00:08:49,193
The purpose is not to become better at defending what you already believe. It is to become capable of understanding what you could not see before.

52
00:08:49,793 --> 00:09:04,260
And we can help one another do this. We can make it possible to question, to admit uncertainty, and to change without humiliation.

53
00:09:06,860 --> 00:09:11,723
[DOT proposes] DOT calls the local experiencer Little c.

54
00:09:12,323 --> 00:09:23,083
[DOT proposes] It proposes that your living body is your interface to a Reality Frame, not the whole of what you are.

55
00:09:23,683 --> 00:09:33,980
[DOT proposes] Think of a musician and an instrument. They work together, but they are not the same thing.

56
00:09:39,780 --> 00:09:55,470
[DOT proposes] This world, RF₀, is modeled as an experience laboratory. You act within its rules, encounter consequences, and carry something of the experience forward.

57
00:09:56,070 --> 00:10:06,850
But experience does not automatically become understanding. What matters is what you do with it.

58
00:10:09,050 --> 00:10:17,393
Conditioning is real. But in DOT’s language, the Painting is not the painter.

59
00:10:17,993 --> 00:10:31,460
You can notice an impulse without immediately obeying it. The conditioning is still there, but your relationship to it can change.

60
00:10:32,060 --> 00:10:43,593
Freedom is participation in the creation of possibility. That includes making room for someone else’s freedom.

61
00:10:44,193 --> 00:10:59,690
This cannot remain private work. We need time to think, enough security to speak honestly, and institutions that do not punish us for questioning them.

62
00:11:00,290 --> 00:11:13,693
That is what I believe a civilization should be for. Helping awareness expand—not making human beings easier to manage.

63
00:11:16,893 --> 00:11:29,000
Digital Organism Theory

64
00:11:19,027 --> 00:11:29,000
A school of thought for examining experience and expanding awareness.

65
00:11:29,400 --> 00:11:42,400
Concept 1 of 17 · The Subjective Data Principle
Feeling must be treated as data, but feeling is not automatically truth.

66
00:11:43,400 --> 00:11:58,400
Concept 2 of 17 · Digital Organism
A living, conscious system that carries its past forward, senses what matters, and adapts to stay whole across change.

67
00:11:59,400 --> 00:12:17,400
Concept 3 of 17 · The Big C Hypothesis
DOT proposes a living, conscious process from which Reality Frames and local experiencers develop.

68
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:36,400
Concept 4 of 17 · Little c
The local experiencer in DOT’s model, embodied by an Avatar and participating through a situated Reality Stream.

69
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:53,400
Concept 5 of 17 · The Decoupling Principle
DOT proposes that Little c and the living body are distinct and functionally connected.

70
00:12:54,400 --> 00:13:11,400
Concept 6 of 17 · Reality Frame
A shared, rule-bound experience laboratory in which Little cs participate through their Avatars.

71
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:29,400
Concept 7 of 17 · Physical Sciences as Frame Derivations
Physics measures RF₀’s regularities; DOT must account for those results within its proposed Frame architecture.

72
00:13:30,400 --> 00:13:48,400
Concept 8 of 17 · Reality Stream
The situated input RF₀ supplies to Little c through the Avatar.

73
00:13:49,400 --> 00:14:01,400
Concept 9 of 17 · Intent
Commitment toward an action, distinct from the thoughts and impulses that remain possible drafts.

74
00:14:02,400 --> 00:14:18,400
Concept 10 of 17 · The Experience Loop
Input is interpreted through the Painting; Intent leads toward action, and consequence updates carried state.

75
00:14:19,400 --> 00:14:34,400
Concept 11 of 17 · Canvas
The capacity to carry and update state, allowing earlier experience to influence what comes next.

76
00:14:35,400 --> 00:14:48,400
Concept 12 of 17 · Painting
The accumulated organization of carried state: expectations, associations, skills, habits, and meanings.

77
00:14:49,400 --> 00:15:01,400
Concept 13 of 17 · Character
The relatively stable patterns expressed through repeated interpretation and action.

78
00:15:02,400 --> 00:15:14,400
Concept 14 of 17 · The Fear-Gating Principle
When Fear governs, the set of responses that feels available becomes narrower.

79
00:15:15,400 --> 00:15:29,400
Ego defends the Character Little c has learned to call itself.
In DOT’s account, Ego protects a familiar self-image. When that defence hardens into Arrogance, unfamiliar experience is more easily dismissed.

80
00:15:30,400 --> 00:15:42,400
Concept 15 of 17 · Love as an Epistemic Condition
Love is the condition in which Fear no longer governs you.

81
00:15:43,400 --> 00:15:57,400
Concept 16 of 17 · Conscious Authorship
Notice inherited interpretation, examine available responses, and act with greater deliberation.

82
00:15:58,400 --> 00:16:13,400
Concept 17 of 17 · Limits and Unpaid Debts
Distinguish observation, model, hypothesis, and speculation, then ask what would support or challenge each claim.
